  ],
  "text": "A laser is only light where every wave is in step. That single change turns it into a tool. A bulb throws out waves of every length, in every direction, all at random. Which is fine for lighting a room and useless for anything that needs precision. A laser puts out one wavelength, one direction, every crest lined up with every other. Which means the beam barely spreads, so you can aim it at something very far away. Cutting metal, reading barcodes, reshaping a cornea, carrying the internet through glass. But the best one is on the Moon. Apollo crews left mirrors up there. We fire a laser at them and time the return. That measures the distance to within millimetres, which is how we know the Moon is drifting away. About four centimetres a year, measured with a beam of light in perfect step. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
